<section aria-label="Technical feasibility" style="box-sizing: border-box; width: 100%; background: #F7F7F4; color: #251F19; font-family: var(--font-text); -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;">
  <div style="box-sizing: border-box; width: 100%; max-width: calc(1280px + 2 * var(--gutter)); margin: 0 auto; padding: clamp(56px, 8vw, 104px) var(--gutter); display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: clamp(32px, 4vw, 48px);">

    <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 18px;">
      <span style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--brand);">03 Technical feasibility</span>
      <h2 style="margin: 0; max-width: 900px; font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 500; font-size: clamp(32px, 3.4vw, 48px); line-height: 1.12; letter-spacing: -0.03em; color: #251F19;">Designing scheduling around what calendar APIs can actually do</h2>
      <p style="margin: 0; max-width: 1120px; font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.65; color: #68615A;">Before designing the interview-scheduling flow, studied how calendar and scheduling APIs (e.g. Google Calendar and Outlook Calendar APIs) work: authentication via OAuth, reading free/busy availability, writing events, and handling two-way sync so a change made in the platform reflects on a hiring manager&#8217;s own calendar and vice versa. This shaped which parts of scheduling could be automated versus which needed a manual confirmation step.</p>
    </div>

